```javascript
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  // Perform n‑1 passes
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    let swapped = false;
    // In each pass, compare adjacent elements up to the unsorted portion
    for (let j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
        const temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
    // If no swaps occurred, the array is already sorted
    if (!swapped) break;
  }
  return arr;
}

// Example usage
const input = [4, 2, 7, 1, 6, 5];
console.log(bubbleSort(input)); // → [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

The algorithm repeatedly steps through the list, swapping any out‑of‑order adjacent pair. After each outer pass the largest remaining element “bubbles” to its final position, so the inner loop can ignore the already‑sorted tail (`n‑1‑i`). The `swapped` flag lets us exit early if the array becomes sorted before all passes finish.

